  "response": "<issues>\n- The use of `np.squeeze` followed by `np.atleast_2d` can unintentionally change the orientation of the data (e.g., turning an `(M, 1, 1)` array into a row vector `(1, M)` instead of a column vector `(M, 1)`), which misrepresents the original tensor's first dimension in the heatmap visualization.\n- Flattening the trailing dimensions via `.reshape(res.shape[0], -1)` is the mathematically correct and standard way to matricize a multi-dimensional array for 2D visualization (mode-1 unfolding), making the complex `squeeze` logic unnecessary and error-prone.\n</issues>\n<solution>\nimport numpy as np\nimport seaborn as sns\n\ndef task_func(P, T):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the product of a matrix 'P' and a 3D tensor 'T' using numpy \n    and visualize the results as a heatmap.\n    \n    Args:\n        P (numpy.ndarray): A 2D matrix.\n        T (numpy.ndarray): A 3D tensor.\n        \n    Returns:\n        tuple:\n            - numpy.ndarray: Resultant product after matrix-tensor multiplication.\n            - matplotlib.axes.Axes: Axes object displaying the heatmap of the 2D result.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Compute the matrix-tensor multiplication (standard mode-1 product)\n    # This multiplies the last axis of P with the first axis of T\n    res = np.tensordot(P, T, axes=1)\n    \n    # Prepare data for the 2D heatmap\n    if res.ndim > 2:\n        # Flatten trailing dimensions to ensure a 2D result (mode-1 matricization)\n        plot_data = res.reshape(res.shape[0], -1)\n    elif res.ndim < 2:\n        # Convert to 2D in case of 1D or 0D results\n        plot_data = np.atleast_2d(res)\n    else:\n        plot_data = res\n            \n    # Visualize the heatmap\n    ax = sns.heatmap(plot_data)\n    \n    return res, ax\n</solution>",
